/** Editorial translations for siman 168 failing blocks (110 blocks) */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#ג': `Forbidden. For since he received it from the hand of the Jew, it appears as giving interest to the non-Jew through his agency; and the essence here is that there is no agency for a non-Jew — neither non-Jew for Jew nor the reverse. Taz. And Siftei Kohen wrote, and it implies that in such a case it is not fixed ribbit, and specifically when it left his hand as a loan; but by way of deposit it is permitted, as below seif 3 in the Hagahah.`,
    '6#_': `With consent. Behold, the non-Jew is the agent of the Jew for stringency and it is forbidden rabbinically. And those who are lenient hold that even for stringency we do not say there is agency for a non-Jew, even in an unsecured debt without collateral. But the Tur brought Hagahos Maimoniyos that they disagree and hold that even according to those who are lenient it is not permitted in this case except with collateral — end quote Taz. And the Tur wrote that even according to this view, if the second Jew says he receives it as the agent of the first, it is forbidden, for he is the agent of the Jew. And Bach wrote that this is specifically when the first Jew made him an agent; otherwise one cannot say he is his agent without his consent. And Siftei Kohen agreed to this, unlike Beit Yosef — see there.`,
    '11#ב': `To acquire for him. Siftei Kohen wrote: But if the borrowing non-Jew says to the lending Jew, give it to so-and-so the non-Jew who will lend to me on it from a Jew with interest, and when you deliver it into his hand I am immediately obligated to return the collateral to you — it is permitted. And Beit Yosef brought in the name of Mordechai in this language: if the non-Jew said to Reuven, place collateral in my place, and the Jew took his collateral and said to Shimon, this collateral belongs to the non-Jew, or said to him, I transfer it to you in his place, and Shimon agreed — it is permitted for him to receive interest from the Jew who pledged, who is Shimon's agent to lend to the non-Jew; and likewise when he transfers collateral to him. And if Reuven denies it, Shimon swears that it is as he said and is exempt — end quote. And Beit Yosef wrote on what he said "this is the non-Jew's collateral" — the permission is even though the collateral belongs to the Jew, because collateral has the law of a guarantor, for he does not sell it unless he first demands that he pay him, etc. And Taz wondered how a holy mouth could say this, for certainly the lender's primary reliance is on the collateral, and what does it help that he demands first that he pay him — in any case payment comes from the Jew, and this is not comparable to a Jew who is guarantor for a non-Jew, for there if the non-Jew demands first payment does not come from the Jew, unlike here. And he extended to explain Mordechai in another matter and disagrees with Beit Yosef and Bach — see there (and Nachalat Yaakov wrote that for R. Meir, since he admitted the collateral belongs to the non-Jew, his admission is like a hundred witnesses and the non-Jew acquired through his admission, and it is comparable to below seif 13 — see there).`,
    '13#ב': `That he say. Siftei Kohen wrote: It appears that whoever did not do so is forbidden to take interest; but Bach wrote that this is specifically lekhatchilah, to avoid Torah prohibition doubt; but certainly if he did not do so it is not ribbit from the default, for here there is only a doubt that perhaps he became the non-Jew's agent, and by default his intent is for permission, and we say he became the second Jew's agent and may take interest if he gives it with his consent or while the collateral is still in his hand. Therefore even if he leaves his collateral with him and says this collateral belongs to the non-Jew, or says to him I transfer it to you in place of the non-Jew — likewise permitted. And it is not specifically acquisition, for there is no acquisition for a non-Jew, but rather such as when he admits it belongs to the non-Jew — in this way the non-Jew acquires it. And if the Jew denies that he transferred it to him, the lender swears as to his words and is exempt to return the collateral to him as long as he does not pay him the interest.`,
    '14#ב': `And there is. And Bach wrote that there is no dispute here; specifically when the non-Jew already redeemed the collaterals and did not give him the interest as the agent stipulated there — then one may not extract from the agent's hand, since he did not explicitly become guarantor for the interest. But when the non-Jew does not redeem except from the agent, and the agent redeems from the lender and says the non-Jew is silent and did not give the interest as he stipulated — the lender is not obligated to return the collateral unless he gives him what he stipulated with him, for he can say I have no dealings with the non-Jew, only with you. And specifically by default; but if the Jew explicitly said you shall be my agent, he must give the collateral without interest, only that he should not suffer loss in principal — end quote. And it is clear from his words that wherever he explicitly stipulated about the interest, obviously he must pay him, for the body of the loan was on the collateral. However, when he mentioned the non-Jew's name and recognized that he is silent, he should not take interest from him even from the collateral, for he knew and waived, and must return the collateral without interest — end quote Siftei Kohen. And Taz wrote: It seems to me, if the agent did not know this non-Jew is coerced and it became known to him afterward, he has no obligation, and the sender must give him the collateral without interest — why should he lose since he did not sin in anything. But if the agent knew the non-Jew is coerced, he is liable under laws of causative damage, for he should have informed the lender that this non-Jew is coerced. And Levush wrote: It seems to me specifically in such a case where the agent brought the collateral with him at the time of the loan; otherwise, if the non-Jew only sent him to bring the money and afterward would give him the collateral — then responsibility for the collateral was never on the agent, for he was only the Jew's agent; the agent can say do not sell it, for it will harm me, that the non-Jew is coerced. And if he sold it and the agent came to harm thereby, the lender must compensate him. Taz disagrees and wrote that whenever the agent knew the non-Jew is coerced and did not inform the lender, he cannot extract the collateral from his hand without interest, for he caused his own loss — end quote. And he further wrote that Rosh explicitly discusses where he sent him to bring collateral from the non-Jew on the lender's responsibility, and nevertheless ruled thus (and Nachalat Yaakov agreed with Levush — see there).`,
    '14#ג': `For the need of. That is, he should say he borrows for the non-Jew's need and not say he will pay principal and interest. And even though it is permitted for a Jew to be guarantor for a non-Jew where there is collateral, as below siman 170 seif 2 — that case differs, for the loan's beginning was to the non-Jew. However, I have seen practiced in many places that the Jew says at the time of the loan that he will pay principal and interest, and it appears they hold that specifically where the collateral is not good to lend on it the full loan amount such that it equals principal and interest that will accrue even over a long time — then it appears the lender's reliance is on the Jew, and in such a case it is forbidden to say so. And in such a case I have truly seen practiced that the Jew gives the lender a promissory note for permission as security. But where the collateral is good, the Jew's words in this neither raise nor lower, since he lends for the non-Jew's need there is no concern for this. Nevertheless one should be careful in the matter — end quote Siftei Kohen.`,
    '18#ד': `They stipulated. Behold, Taz and Siftei Kohen wonder at this law, since they stipulated from the outset that he must return and give it to him; and likewise where he is obligated to return by custom, which is as explicit stipulation — if so this is not a sale and it is forbidden to take interest from him, as the Rabbi wrote below siman 174. And likewise it is explicit in Mordechai that it is permitted only because since he is not obligated to return the collateral to him, it is a sale; therefore Taz disagrees with this Hagahah and brings several proofs to refute Rama's words, and concludes it appears to be stringent, and every conscientious person should act so that it be with the second lender's willingness to return to redeem it if he redeems without the non-Jew's knowledge — end quote (and likewise in responsum Pnei Yehoshua siman 4). And Siftei Kohen wrote: But in truth it seems to me that the custom is simply to lend on non-Jewish collaterals, and lenders also return collaterals to the Jew — meaning they do so willingly, as if he returned and sold it to him by default, but they are not obligated to do so. And even if you say they are obligated, one can say this is because they relied on what Rosh wrote, that a non-Jew's collateral in a Jew's hand with interest — it is permitted to pledge it to another Jew at that interest even though they did not present it before the non-Jew, and we do not say it turns out the Jew takes interest, for he did not acquire it except where he said to him from now, for then his intent is to remove it. And therefore it discusses where he does not sell it to him, and it is a case where the second Jew is obligated to return it to the first Jew to redeem it — meaning they stipulated thus, or in a place where the custom is so. But if it discusses selling to him in a complete sale, or pledging it with him by default, as one who said I sell it to you — for by default he intends for permission — if so, even if the non-Jew said acquire from now, likewise permitted, for it should only be truly the Jew's; permitted to sell to a Jew and then the Jew returns and sells it to him as below. Rather it certainly discusses only by way of collateral in the second Jew's hand, and nevertheless permitted, for since the non-Jew did not say acquire from now unless I redeem it by such-and-such time, as stated — end quote.`,
    '19#_': `It is the trickery of the wicked. To make a permit because the collateral belongs to the non-Jew — and it does not help, since the Jew fixed interest for him. And Bach wrote that it is already explained: if he said about the collaterals that they belong to the non-Jew, even though he gives interest it is permitted. And it seems to me specifically when he said to him, this collateral of the non-Jew — lend on it thus and thus, and when the non-Jew comes to redeem it take such-and-such interest — we say by default he intended for permission and he removes himself from the collateral. But if he said to him, lend me money with interest on these collaterals that belong to the non-Jew — certainly it is fixed ribbit, for he fixed interest with him whether the non-Jew gives interest or not; what difference does it make if the collateral belonged to the non-Jew, since the Jew fixed interest for him. And obviously in such a case they did not practice leniency. And even though below siman 170 we say it is permitted for a Jew to be guarantor where there is collateral — here, where the Jew lent first for his own need, it is forbidden. However, all this is when the collateral was removed to the Jew; but when it is in the non-Jew's possession and he did not say from now — in every case permitted — end quote Siftei Kohen. And Derishah wrote that if the first Jew lent on the collateral one hundred and wants to pledge it in the second Jew's hand for fifty — then the collateral is still in his possession and he returns and borrows on it for his need — forbidden. And Taz wrote he was not precise in this, for Tosafot explicitly wrote there is no distinction — see there (and Nachalat Yaakov agreed with Derishah — see there).`,
    '25#ב': `Knows. Siftei Kohen wrote: The same applies if he says "I do not know" — he must swear a heses oath, as the Mechaber wrote below siman 177 seif 1; and likewise Bach and Semag in CM siman 82 s.k. 34, unlike those who disagree in the Hagahah siman 177 there. And Taz wrote: Nevertheless one must understand why he is truly obligated to swear in these claims more than in the claim "I took it permissibly," for since if he admitted he took from him he would still have a claim to exempt himself that he took permissibly — and we find in CM siman 93 seif 9 that we administer a heses oath only on a claim where if he admitted he would be liable for money, etc. And it appears that it does not suffice to say "I took it permissibly" without specification, but he must specify the permission — how it was — lest he not know how to make a permit. Therefore if he explicitly says it was done permissibly as we can investigate him, if it is a matter where there is no concern it did not occur to him that he would need a permit — therefore if he says "I do not know" or is silent, there is this concern, and therefore he swears he does not know or is silent — end quote.`,
    '25#ד': `Collateral. Siftei Kohen wrote: It implies even if he claims "you lent me one hundred with fixed ribbit on collateral" and the other responds "I acted permissibly with you" — he is exempt without oath; and all the more if he claims "I left collateral with you for interest" and the other claims it was done permissibly from the outset. And that which was written above seif 18, that when one claims "I pledged collateral with you at fixed ribbit" and the other responds "you told me it belongs to the non-Jew" and he denies it — the lender swears, etc. — one can distinguish: here it is permitted because we do not abandon a permitted path and eat forbidden matter, and we say he certainly acted permissibly; but there he did nothing permissibly, only says he relied on you that you said it belongs to the non-Jew — if so the essence of the permit is from the borrower's word, and the borrower denies him, therefore he must swear. And if the lender comes to extract payment, the borrower swears and is exempt — this is puzzling, for here it rules the borrower swears even though he makes himself and the lender wicked; and below siman 177 seif 2 it wrote that even though if he wishes he exempts himself by saying "I paid" or "I do not know" and would swear and be exempt — he is not believed even with an oath, for a person does not make himself wicked; and the matters contradict each other — puzzling — end quote.`,
    '25#ה': `The lender. And one can say regarding above siman 162, that there one cannot say "he did not abandon a permitted path," etc., for here there is certainly prohibition; but there it did not enter his mind that wheat would become expensive so he would be careful to have wheat at the time of the loan. And Semag wrote in CM siman 82 s.k. 33: specifically here where he admits there is ribbit but says it was done permissibly — he swears and takes by promissory note as the law of anyone who swears on a document and takes. But if the lender stands on his document and says "I do not know" that there is any ribbit at all — he need not swear before giving money by promissory note, but pays and afterward sues him and administers a heses oath. See in responsum Maharashdam siman 67 on laws relevant here — end quote Siftei Kohen. And Taz wrote that Rama decided thus from his own view that on a document one swears and takes — end quote (and Nachalat Yaakov challenged him — see there).`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '3#_': `Rosh in a responsum, klal 108 siman 13.`,
    '12#_': `There in the entry "such as," in the name of Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam; and likewise Tosafot in the name of Rashi; and likewise Rambam in perek 5 of Hilchos Malveh; and Hagahos Maimoniyos wrote that this is also the view of Ramban and Rashba.`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '9#main': `A non-Jew who borrowed money from a Jew with interest on collateral, and at the time of redeeming the collateral a Jew came and said the collateral is mine — the lender is not believed, and witnesses cannot help him testify that those very coins the lender gave the non-Jew returned and were given to the Jew, for he can say he withheld them for himself and lent him from his own money. And if he knows the collateral belongs to the Jew, it is forbidden to lend on it; and even if he does not know for certain, if it is known that it belongs to the Jew, such as a garment that non-Jews do not customarily wear — it is unseemly to take interest. {Rama: And all this is only for one who holds there is agency for a non-Jew for stringency; but for one who does not hold so, it is permitted in every matter (Beit Yosef). Therefore they wrote in the name of Rabbeinu Tam that it is permitted to give collaterals to a non-Jew from the outset, even his slave and maidservant, to pledge them with another Jew, even if they are his books and the lender recognizes them; and even if the lender returns and deposits them with the borrower it is permitted, since a non-Jew's hand is intermediary — there is no agency for a non-Jew (Beit Yosef in the name of Mordechai, perek Avodah Zarah, and in Hagahos Ashiri in the name of Aviasaf, and Tur, and Hagahos Maimoniyos, and Nimukei Yosef). And I already wrote that one should not protest those who practice leniency; and even if the non-Jew goes and the Jew redeems the collateral it is permitted. However, if the lender does not wish to give the collateral to the Jew whom the non-Jew sent, the authority is in his hand, for he has dealings only with the non-Jew (responsum of Rosh klal 108 siman 8, and so is implied in the poskim). Therefore initially the collateral must also be equal to his money so that he should have no dealings with the borrower at all if he wishes (so is implied there).}`,
    '13#main': `A non-Jew who said to a Jew, lend me money from a Jew with interest on my collateral — it is permitted for the lender to take interest from the Jew the agent, provided the lender says to the agent: you shall be my agent to bring me the collateral from the non-Jew on my responsibility and to convey the money to him on my responsibility. {Rama: And if the non-Jew gave the collateral to the Jew before the second Jew lent — even if responsibility for the collateral was on the agent it is permitted (the Rabbi's reasoning, unlike Beit Yosef), only that responsibility for the money be on the lender. And likewise at redemption: you shall be my agent to bring my money from the non-Jew and they shall be on my responsibility from when they leave the non-Jew's hand; and likewise you shall be my agent to return the collateral to me. And if he does not trust him he should say so in his heart and need not inform him, or give it to him with a condition that he not be believed to say it was lost or stolen except before witnesses. And if he did so and at redemption the agent says, you did not tell me the truth initially but it is mine — the lender says, I do not believe you except in your first words I believe, but I am concerned for your later words and do not wish to accept money from you; and when the non-Jew comes and gives me the money I will give him his collateral. And even if he wishes to swear he is not believed; and even if witnesses testify it belongs to the Jew it does not help, since he first admitted it is not his. Rama: And all the more so a borrower's oath does not help (Beit Yosef in the name of an Ashkenazic responsum). However, if the matter became known that so is the truth, it is forbidden to take interest from him; and one who schemes to do so is called wicked (Beit Yosef in the name of Rashba). And all this discusses only lending on collateral; but if he gave him money to lend to the non-Jew with interest and afterward the recipient said, I did not lend to the non-Jew but took for my own need — the recipient is believed that he took for his own need; and if he is suspected of lying, he is excommunicated that his words are true; and one who does so is called wicked (responsum of Rashba siman 453).}`,
    '14#main': `A Jew who borrowed from his fellow for the non-Jew's need and fixed with the non-Jew interest at a higher rate, and with the lending Jew fixed at a lower rate — the borrower profits what he fixed with the non-Jew more than what he fixed with the lending Jew. But if he fixed with the lender at a higher rate and the non-Jew is silent and unwilling to pay them, the borrower should not pay from his own pocket (Mordechai and Hagahos Ashiri). {Rama: And some say the borrower must pay from his pocket unless he mentioned the non-Jew's name to him initially — then he is exempt from paying from his pocket (Tur and Mordechai and Hagahos Ashiri in the name of Avi HaEzri). And in any case, lekhatchilah it is forbidden to borrow on a non-Jew's collateral and say he will pay principal and interest, for it is as borrowing from a Jew and relending to a non-Jew; rather the loan's beginning must be for the non-Jew's need. If the agent comes to redeem the collateral from his own money, it is permitted to accept principal and interest from him (Beit Yosef in the name of a responsum of Rashba). However, specifically when the lender gives it to him to redeem willingly; but if the agent redeems from his own funds it is deception, for from the outset he lent him on condition the collateral remain in his hand until the non-Jew redeems it (Hagahos Ashiri and Beit Yosef in the name of a responsum of Rosh). And if the agent does not wish to redeem it and the lender wishes them to redeem it, he cannot compel him, as will be explained at the end of this siman (Beit Yosef).}`,
    '19#main': `A Jew who comes to his fellow and says, lend me money on these collaterals that belong to a non-Jew, and he lent him and the money is for the Jew's need and he pays him interest from his own, or says to his fellow, lend me money with interest and I will send you collateral through a non-Jew, and the non-Jew brought the collateral to the lender and conveyed the money to the borrower — this is the trickery of the wicked, and he should not take any interest at all.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '4#_': `(Taz s.k. 17) And I wonder that this contradicts, etc. For R. Meir — in seif 4 it discusses where the non-Jew lent to the second Jew as agent of the first Jew, as Siftei Kohen s.k. 12 wrote — see there.`,
    '6#_': `(Taz s.k. 20) He challenged Bach and Pri Megadim improperly, for what the Tur wrote in the name of Rosh "he will add collateral" is a standalone matter as Beit Yosef wrote, but it does not refer to the beginning where afterward the collateral is on the lender; and proof — this law is in Rosh's responsum klal 108 siman 7, and there it was not mentioned that responsibility is on the lender — see there.`,
    '10#_': `(Taz s.k. 37) Rather Rama decided, etc. This is not that Rama wrote thus in Darkei Moshe in the name of Beit Yosef, as Siftei Kohen s.k. 73 wrote — see there. Also what he wrote "and it appears thus is proven from what is stated in perek HaRibbit," etc. — he was not precise, for there it is a ribbit document; but here he claims "I took it permissibly," and likewise what he wrote that Rama learned from that CM matter — is not a valid point, for there he does not swear and take but pays and afterward swears an oath after payment; and further, specifically there where he claims he did not take at all, unlike here where he claims "I took it permissibly" — one can say he did not abandon a permitted path and eat forbidden matter, as Siftei Kohen wrote — see there.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `The interest into the hand. Taz — not specifically interest but principal as well; and see in responsum Beit Yaakov siman 17 what he wrote on this.`,
    '5#_': `It is as if they stipulated thus. See in Semag CM siman 72 s.k. 114 who wrote that all this is as long as the non-Jew is alive, for then the first Jew stands in the non-Jew's place to redeem him; but if the non-Jew died, the second Jew acquired the collateral, for since the non-Jew's power departed, so too departed what comes from his power. However, if the non-Jew has heirs who know of this and intend to redeem it, then the first Jew too can redeem it, etc. And see in responsum Tzitz Eliezer siman 20 who disagrees; his view is that always the second Jew must give to redeem the first Jew, because it is not a sale but only for ribbit permission and not to acquire truly — see there.`,
    '6#_': `And in unsecured debts. Siftei Kohen; and see in responsum Avnei Nezer Panim Me'irot part 2 siman 22 and siman 34.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Simanim 168–169, Siftei Kohen s.k. 12) Except when the borrower acted as agent. I do not understand — behold Rama wrote "but if the non-Jew has collateral and it was not done as agency" — it implies explicitly that even without agency we require that he have collateral. And likewise from Siftei Kohen's own words (s.k. 14) in what he wrote "but for Rabbeinu Tam, even if done as agency it is permitted" — it is clear that this matter of "not done as agency" discusses where he has collateral — puzzling.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 7) And the borrower does a prohibition. So too Rosh in a responsum (klal 108 siman 11). And in his rulings he quoted R. Yitzchak's language that there is no prohibition for the lender. However, in a responsum (there siman 18) he quoted R. Yitzchak's words that there is no prohibition neither for lender nor borrower. And likewise it was copied in Be'er HaGolah. Puzzling.`,
    '5#_': `(Taz s.k. 20) Since both know of the matter. I wonder — if so, where the lender did not know, the permission of acquisition stands; if so, why do we need the reasoning that they are not believed — behold, since initially he did not know, even though it became clear it belongs to the Jew, nevertheless it was permitted through acquisition. And more so, Siftei Kohen wrote in truth (s.k. 26) that even though it is now known to him it is permitted because of acquisition. If so, why did the Mechaber write the permitted case that they are not believed. Also I wonder at the foundation of Beit Yosef and Derishah that for Rabbeinu Tam in any case it is permitted; if so it implies for Rabbeinu Tam, since there is no agency for a non-Jew, we need not the reasoning of acquisition. And since so, also for Rashi's view, where he did not know and it became clear afterward — we need not acquisition reasoning nor the reasoning that they are not believed, for we hold like R. Yitzchak that in unsecured debt without the lender's knowledge it is permitted to take interest even for Rashi's view as above (seif 7). And this is that where he did not know they were not stringent to say there is agency for a non-Jew. If so, automatically where he did not know there is no difference between Rabbeinu Tam's view and Rashi's. And just as for Rabbeinu Tam where he knows it is permitted and acquisition reasoning is not needed, so too for Rashi where he did not know. And see Bach who wrote in truth that the Tur comes to say that even for those who forbid, according to Rashi, even where he did not know — nevertheless the collateral is permitted because of acquisition. If so, for us too, even for Rashi's view acquisition is not needed. Also in this foundation that for Rabbeinu Tam in any case it is permitted — and Taz also wrote thus in Shulchan Aruch (seif 10): if the pledged party did not know, etc., and this is from Mahariik that in this there is no aspect of agency — it implies that without an aspect of agency there is no prohibition with collateral. And Taz also wrote Siftei Kohen (s.k. 26) that for Rabbeinu Tam and his companions it is permitted; behold, even though there is no acquisition there, nevertheless it is permitted for Rabbeinu Tam. And in my humble opinion, from Ramban's words in Be'er HaGolah siman 66 ch. 4 it implies it is not from agency that it is forbidden, but rather that whenever the collateral was not acquired by the non-Jew and its responsibility is the lender's on the collateral — it is forbidden. And Taz forbids also in the second case of agency where he commanded to pledge to the non-Jew and pledged to a Jew, for in such a case he did not acquire for him. And likewise is explained in Nimukei Yosef (and see Darkei Moshe — puzzling); and as Ramban's language there is precise: "and a Jew's collateral in a non-Jew's hand — if a non-Jew borrows on it from a Jew with interest, it is forbidden" — end quote; it implies in every case. And even for Rabbeinu Tam that there is no agency for a non-Jew — nevertheless it is forbidden from the aspect of collateral, for ribbit extends on the Jew's collateral — see there. Examine well.`,
    '8#_': `(Siftei Kohen s.k. 40) And likewise it appears from Rashba's responsum. In my humble opinion there is no proof from there, for there it discusses a broker who cannot obligate others through his admission. And likewise Maharshal wrote plainly in a responsum there. And even if only one witness says it belongs to the Jew, it is forbidden for him to take interest, for one witness is believed in prohibitions; but the broker himself — either from one witness or from the law of one witness, since his words contradict each other. Beit Yosef in the name of Rashba's responsum.`,
    '11#_': `(There s.k. 45) But he is not called wicked. And in my humble opinion one can say simply: above he is called wicked since the law would be that the lender can say I do not believe you and he would have to pay; and since the truth is the collateral is his, he transgresses "do not charge interest." Unlike here, he did nothing — behold in truth he will not pay, and he is only in the category of "the remnant of Israel shall not do," etc. However, I wonder why he is not completely wicked, for he is misappropriating his fellow's property — he only gave it to lend to the non-Jew, and in what he held for himself he is misappropriating — puzzling.`,
    '13#_': `(Siftei Kohen s.k. 52) And according to Ran. This is puzzling — for also according to Ran it is permitted for a Jew to be a contractor on behalf of a Jew who borrowed from a non-Jew, as is clear below (siman 170) in Beit Yosef. And Bach already wondered thus on Beit Yosef in responsum Lechem Rav and in responsum Mahari Halevi, brother of Taz (siman 5).`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#ב': `And even if he wrote, etc., and gave, etc. Responsum of Rosh; and from Tosafot cited above, since they only found permission when he presented him.`,
    '3#ג': `And if the second Jew, etc. So wrote Nimukei Yosef, and Beit Yosef brought it; and their words are puzzling, for on the contrary there it is written the opposite — that he explained if they presented him they made the first Jew a guardian, etc., therefore it is permitted. Nevertheless we establish in the Gemara specifically when he placed them on the ground, etc., as stated; and the Acharonim already challenged this — see Shach.`,
    '5#א': `If a Jew, etc. This is from Rashba's responsum that Beit Yosef brought siman 130 side 2 entry "if a Jew," etc.; and he wrote: I wonder at this, for in siman 66 "a non-Jew borrowed," etc., it is permitted — for here he is scheming; and further, if he presented him, etc., it is forbidden because of agency for stringency as Shulchan Aruch wrote there, and Shulchan Aruch did not write here such a case which is through scheming; and this is what the Hagahah says "and specifically," etc., and "according to the one who says," etc.; and their disputes will be explained in siman 66.`,
    '5#ב': `And even if, etc., but, etc. So Hagahos Maimoniyos, and Darkei Moshe brought it s.k. 18; and see Mordechai siman 356 who wrote: a non-Jew borrowed money from a Jew, etc., even though the lender knows the non-Jew borrows for his fellow Jew's need — he collects principal and interest, for there is no agency for a non-Jew — see there. And he wrote: I found the words of a great one who disagrees with Rabbeinu Tam; and his proof from what is taught in Tosafot: a non-Jew who appointed a Jew as overseer or guardian — forbidden to borrow from him with interest; and he wrote: it seems we can answer that overseer and guardian are a well-known matter that his loan is for the Jew; and therefore here because it appears, etc.; but all poskim explain because it is on the Jew's responsibility, as stated "this is the rule," etc., and as stated below siman 64. (Likkut) And even if, etc. So Nimukei Yosef, because he was challenged on Rabbeinu Tam's ruling from Tosafot that teaches there: a Jew who said to a non-Jew, go borrow my money with interest — forbidden; and he distinguishes between with collateral or without — see there siman 33; and see Mordechai siman 347. But the poskim distinguished between on the Jew's responsibility or the gentile's (end quote).`,
    '6#א': `And even if he said to him, etc. Rosh there — since they only forbade when he presented him, as stated; and likewise Tosafot there s.v. metzao.`,
    '9#א': `A non-Jew borrowed, etc. Rosh there; and further he wrote in a responsum "if he gave," etc.; and in Sefer HaTerumah and Tur and Shulchan Aruch — that Ramban disagrees with R. Yitzchak that we do not say by default he transferred it to him; but Rosh holds it is permitted only that the lender should not know the collateral belongs to the Jew and that he borrows for a Jew's need; and if a Jew says it is mine they are not believed, etc.; but if he knows, etc., as written here — all this is in the Tur according to R. Yitzchak's words in the Rishonim in his rulings siman 55; and R. Yitzchak wrote in a responsum to the Terumat HaDeshen: even if you say, etc., as stated in responsum klal 108 siman 18; and all this is according to Rashi as stated in his rulings and responsum there — and there; and therefore "and all this is not," etc.`,
    '9#ב': `Therefore he wrote in the name of Rabbeinu Tam, etc. Tosafot s.v. kegon and in a responsum, etc.; and Hagahos Ashiri siman 57 entry "it appears," etc.`,
    '9#ד': `And even if the non-Jew goes, etc. Hagahos Ashiri there; and Rashba wrote, etc.; and likewise siman 66; and even if he said to him "I," etc.; and for Rabbeinu Tam even if he presented him, as stated.`,
    '10#א': `A non-Jew who pledged, etc. So Semak and Tur in his name — that even according to R. Yitzchak cited above, that if the Jew gave his collateral to borrow on it it is permitted because he transfers acquisition to the non-Jew; but in such a case where he pledged against his will, why need this; and it is like the view of Baal HaItur that Rosh brought in perek 2 of Pesachim; but according to Rosh who disagreed with him it is permitted, as stated above in siman 20; and as Tur wrote here; and from the words of Rosh z"l below it appears this too is permitted; and as Rosh wrote in Pesachim — the prohibition is on borrower and lender, and he did not lend to the Jew at all but to the non-Jew, and R. Yitzchak and Ramban's words do not apply at all, for there the Jew sends the non-Jew to borrow from a Jew and requires the reasoning of transfer; and as Rosh wrote here; but there where the non-Jew borrows for his own need Rosh permitted there; and likewise he distinguishes in Sefer HaTerumah folio 65 side 4; except Semak's view — since he took collateral from the Jew and immediately went and pledged to another Jew, it appears as borrowing for a Jew's need; and as Beit Yosef wrote folio 130 side 4 entry "and from the words of Rosh," etc., because one can say, etc.; and therefore "and likewise if the Jew," etc. — agency does not apply at all since he did not know; and specifically if the Jew himself redeems it, for then it appears from the outset he was his agent; and therefore "if Israel," etc.; unlike below seif 20 where he wrote even if a Jew redeems it is permitted; and here by default is like Rosh's view there; but what Beit Yosef wrote entry above "or else there the Jew," etc. — his words are puzzling, for Baal HaItur's reason is that a non-Jew does not acquire collateral; if so Rosh should have refuted his words that in truth he transferred it; and he challenged him that in lender and borrower, etc.; and further, did the Jew know from the outset the non-Jew would need to borrow on it — and you can challenge that by default the Jew's intent is to transfer; if so regarding chametz too; and all this is according to Rashi in siman 66 whom the poskim agreed with, as stated.`,
    '11#ו': `And if the non-Jew, etc. For in such a case there is no lender's name on the non-Jew, as stated in Bava Kamma 97a; and Abaye, etc.; and Taz.`,
    '11#ז': `A non-Jew, etc. As stated in Bava Metzia 34a — he transfers double to him. Mordechai and Atzei Chaim.`,
    '13#א': `A non-Jew, etc. As stated in Tosafeta perek 5: a Jew who said to a non-Jew, here is your wage, go and lend my money with interest — forbidden; and a non-Jew who said to a Jew, take your wage and lend my money with interest — permitted, but forbidden because of marit ayin. A Jew's money deposited with a non-Jew — permitted to lend with interest; and a non-Jew's deposited with a Jew — forbidden to lend with interest. A Jew who became guardian or overseer for a non-Jew — permitted to borrow from him with interest; and a non-Jew who became guardian or overseer for a Jew — forbidden to borrow from him with interest. This is the rule: whatever is on the Jew's responsibility is forbidden; whatever is on the non-Jew's responsibility is permitted. And likewise in Yerushalmi — the first case means "take your wage," and the last cases of guardian and overseer are on the depositor's responsibility; and the middle case of deposited money is on the depositary's responsibility; and in the last case, since it is well known, even marit ayin does not apply — see below end of this siman. And where a Jew became guardian and overseer — it is permitted to lend to a Jew who became the Jew's agent to borrow from a non-Jew, since responsibility is not on him; likewise he becomes borrower of the Jew to lend to the non-Jew; and therefore "and he should say," etc.; and likewise, etc.; and see Mordechai siman 347 and 348 and Rosh in his rulings siman 56; and a gentile who said, etc.; and Tosafeta, etc.; and in Rosh's responsum and Tur wrote in the name of Ramban that one need not accept except responsibility for the money and not for the collateral; but Rosh disagrees and requires responsibility for the collateral too — see there.`,
    '13#ג': `And if he did, etc., he should say, etc. And even though one witness is believed in prohibitions — here since their words contradict each other he is not believed; and greater than this we say in Gittin 54b: Rava said, such as if he found him, etc. — all the more here where he says the opposite. Rashba and Avnei Nezer 131 side 3.`,
    '13#ה': `And even if, etc. In Mordechai and Shulchan Aruch he wrote in the name of Rivba: since the borrower is seized in collateral he is not believed with his oath to extract from his hand; and therefore in the Hagahah "and all this does not discuss," etc.; but this does not come to Shulchan Aruch's conclusion; and even witnesses who testify, etc.; and all the more according to the reason he wrote that a person does not make himself wicked — and here he transgressed from the time of deposit; if so obviously witnesses are believed; and likewise even orally he is not believed except here he speaks Rosh's words in a responsum: because admission of one party is like a hundred witnesses, and a person is believed, etc., as stated in Keritut 12a and Shevuot 41b "go bring witnesses," etc.; "whoever says," etc. Nevertheless it fits well what the Hagahah wrote "and all this," etc., because there one can say "I was joking with you," unlike with collateral; and in Avnei Nezer siman 81 s.k. 4; and see below siman 177.`,
    '13#ו': `However if, etc. As stated in Kiddushin 66a: Rava, if you believe me, etc. There.`,
    '14#ה': `If the agent, etc. As stated above siman 66; and even if he said to him "I," etc., for the sake of, etc.`,
    '16#א': `A Jew, etc. Tur in the name of Ramban; and as stated above seif 13; and as stated in Sefer HaTerumah that what Tosafeta and Yerushalmi mean by "on responsibility" is like a borrower, but as guardian he is like an agent without responsibility — see below siman 161 in Taz and Siftei Kohen in the name of Sefer HaTerumah.`,
    '17#א': `A Jew, etc. Rosh in his rulings siman 56; and as stated above seif 13 in Tosafeta; and Rosh brought it there.`,
    '17#ב': `Some say, etc., that it is like, etc. See in CM siman 72 seif 5.`,
    '17#ג': `Even though there is no, etc. As Rashba wrote there: your guarantor is a gentile — who will tell us the guardian is permitted thus — see there; and Beit Yosef brought it at the end of the siman.`,
    '18#א': `His collateral, etc., provided, etc. As stated regarding double in the beginning of perek HaMafkid — "becomes," etc. Rosh in a responsum and Mordechai; and therefore in the Hagahah "and some say," etc., as stated "becomes." (Likkut) His collateral, etc. Rosh in Bava Metzia there; and unlike what is written in perek 2 of Pesachim in the name of Baal HaItur; and he agreed with him that it is permitted because a Jew does not acquire collateral from a non-Jew, as stated; and it implies he need not sell it to him (end quote).`,
    '18#ד': `And it is possible, etc. As stated siman 174 in the Hagahah — see there.`,
    '18#ז': `Also if, etc., provided, etc. As stated in that Tosafeta "this is the rule," etc.; and in the Gemara 72b: the Sages taught "one who conveyed," etc., "with permission," etc.`,
    '18#ט': `And some say, etc., that words of Torah, etc. As stated in perek 4 (46b, 47a, 48a) and like R. Yochanan.`,
    '18#י': `Nevertheless in such a case, etc. Meaning as stated in Sefer HaTerumah ch. 4 folio 66 side 3 that they were lenient in the matter because of livelihood, etc.; and one should not rely on this, as Rashba wrote one must be stringent in ribbit; and as stated "granted the latter case is stringent" even though there is no agency for a non-Jew by Torah law; and further that here it is Torah ribbit law, since the Sages annulled acquisition of money, as Tosafot Avodah Zarah 63a s.v. veha wrote — see there.`,
    '18#נ': `And forbidden, etc. As stated siman 65 side 2; and as stated siman 174.`,
    '18#פ': `You exempt, etc. As stated in the Hagahah — see there; and all the more regarding ribbit that it is permitted; and Tosafot Avodah Zarah 2b s.v. bishlama, etc.; and even if the Jew did not renounce, etc.`,
    '19#_': `A Jew, etc., trickery, etc. From the time of appraisal he transgressed, as stated siman 62 side 1.`,
    '20#א': `And some forbid. From what is stated in perek 2 of Pesachim (31b) that a non-Jew does not acquire collateral from a Jew; and as stated above seif 10.`,
    '20#ג': `And if the non-Jew has, etc., if there is not, etc. Rashi 67b s.v. be'atra demesalki, etc.; and in the Gemara there.`,
    '21#_': `And if he lent, etc. So Rashba explained what he wrote "and if they were on the non-Jew's responsibility" — meaning in such a case.`,
    '22#_': `His money, etc., but forbidden, etc. As stated below siman 23; and as stated in Tosafeta.`,
    '23#ב': `However, etc. As stated below siman 24; and as stated above seif 13 — see there.`,
    '25#ג': `And if there is, etc. His words are puzzling, for a document equals collateral; and the law of prozbul is certainly without collateral, for collateral otherwise is not released.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '3#א': `"He placed them on the ground and exempted," etc. So it is in the Gemara and all poskim; and the Mechaber follows his reasoning that he wrote in Beit Yosef: since Rambam and Tur did not mention "and exempt," apparently in every case it is permitted. And to me it appears that "and exempt" is specifically required; and further, for even if he said "throw my debt to me," behold it is on the borrower's responsibility until he says "and exempt," as explained in CM siman 120 — see there. And from Rambam there is no proof at all, for he did not mention this law of placing on the ground at all — one could challenge him why he did not mention "and exempt"; and this is because the Gemara challenges "what is there to say — it is obvious," and establishes it in another resolution of giving and taking by hand; therefore Rambam did not mention the resolution of placing on the ground and exempting for the simple meaning; and on the contrary it is proven apparently the opposite — if Rambam held "and exempt" is not specifically required, he should have written it to inform us it is not specifically required. And from Tur too there is no proof, for he refers to the beginning where he said "give them to this Jew and be exempt" — forbidden; and he says in the conclusion that if he said "place them on the ground" in place of "give them to him," meaning he said place them on the ground and exempt — permitted. And further I found in an old Tur it was written "and exempt," and it is clear to me this is a omission in other books; I again saw in Bach that he wanted to prove from the Gemara that "and exempt" is not specifically required — and his words are puzzling; for what he wrote "since the Gemara does not establish when he said place on the ground without exempt," etc. — on the contrary, to the Gemara it is obvious it is forbidden; and what he wrote "further this is a fortiori," etc. — it is a fortiori challenge; for when he gave and took by hand the first Jew departed and behold it is in the non-Jew's possession; but when he placed on the ground, perhaps he placed them saying they shall be in your possession — he said.`,
    '5#א': `"And specifically," etc. And in Rashba's responsum that Beit Yosef brought it implies that where it was done intentionally to hang his money in a non-Jew's hand through scheming in order to lend to a Jew in every matter — it is forbidden; and the Rabbi discusses where it was done unintentionally without scheming.`,
    '5#ד': `"But if the non-Jew has collateral," etc., "that he need not coerce," etc. — that the collateral is good and equals principal and profit, as below seif 9 in the Hagahah.`,
    '7#ב': `"And some are lenient." There is no prohibition for borrower nor lender even to take interest from a Jew even if he knew; however this is specifically in a matter that does not appear like ribbit, as above seif 5 in the Hagahah.`,
    '9#ה': `"However if the lender," etc., "the authority is in his hand." Specifically in such a case where the Jew initially gave it to pledge; but if the Jew lent his vessels to the non-Jew, or even to pledge them temporarily and return them to him — then the non-Jew who pledged them to a Jew for a term is a robber, and the second Jew must return them to the first, as Mordechai wrote — he is like a robber who took from this one and gave to that one (CM siman 369); and likewise Bach. And Beit Yosef and the Rabbi below seif 11 distinguish regarding one witness.`,
    '11#א': `"Permitted." Even if the lender knew it belongs to the Jew — for he transferred it to the non-Jew and acquired it through meshichah.`,
    '13#א': `"On my collateral." Beit Yosef wrote: from Nimukei Yosef and Ran's words it appears likewise without collateral it is permitted when the lender has no responsibility on this agent — such as when he informed him that so-and-so the non-Jew borrowed from him, or they presented him before the non-Jew and the agent was exempted thereby — end quote; and he further wrote in the name of Beit HaTalmud; and he wrote the same law when he says to him how much you take from the non-Jew give me — permitted; and if he does not trust him he swears he did not give the non-Jew more and is exempt. But if he said to him as long as my money is in the non-Jew's hand you give me a dinar per month — forbidden until here; and he brings Darkei Moshe — see there; and see in responsum Maharam Galanti siman 4; and see below siman 170 s.k. 8.`,
    '13#ג': `"And he should say," etc. It appears that whoever did not do so is forbidden to take interest; but Bach wrote this is specifically lekhatchilah to avoid Torah prohibition doubt; but certainly if he did not do so it is not ribbit from the default, for here there is only a doubt that perhaps he became the non-Jew's agent, and by default his intent is for permission, and we say he became the second Jew's agent and may take interest if he gives with his consent or while collateral is still in his hand. Therefore even if he leaves his collateral and says this collateral belongs to the non-Jew, or says I transfer it to you in his place — likewise permitted for the non-Jew too. And it is not specifically acquisition, for there is no acquisition for a non-Jew, but rather meaning in a way the non-Jew acquires — such as when he admits it belongs to the non-Jew — end quote. And Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Hagahos Ashiri: if the Jew denies he did not transfer it to him, the lender swears as to his words and is exempt to return the collateral as long as he does not pay him interest. And likewise Bach siman 13 — see there; and Pri Megadim wrote the same when he denies the lender did not say it belongs to the non-Jew; and likewise below s.k. 44.`,
    '13#ה': `"The lender should say," etc. And it implies even an oath is not needed; and one can say unlike below s.k. 54, for here the borrower does not contradict to swear but says I believe your first words; and likewise Darkei Moshe seif 17 wrote; except he wrote and so it appears in Rosh's responsum klal 108 siman 9. However in Rosh's responsum siman 7 it implies the borrower is believed; and possibly these are two views, for Rosh did not sign these responsa — end quote. And it is puzzling, for in siman 7 he wrote the law Shulchan Aruch wrote siman 162, and Rosh himself distinguishes a proper distinction there; and I brought it below s.k. 77.`,
    '13#ז': `"It does not help since he first admitted," etc. For a person is believed about himself more than a hundred witnesses; and Maharshal in a responsum siman 99 wrote: where it is clarified by witnesses that it is his, it is forbidden, and admission of one party like a hundred witnesses does not apply in such a case — see there his reason; and likewise it appears from Rashba's responsum that Beit Yosef brought; and see in responsum Mahari Beit siman 2 siman 132 folio 64 side 4; and Maharshal further wrote there: where his wife pledged the collaterals even though she conducts business in the house in all matters and says it belonged to a non-Jew — he must return the collaterals if it is clarified they are his for all; and likewise if he cannot clarify and claims he himself knows the collaterals are his — he must swear (that he) [is not] knowing — see there.`,
    '14#ב': `"And some say he must," etc. And Bach wrote there is no dispute; specifically when the non-Jew already redeemed the collaterals and did not give him interest as the agent stipulated — then one may not extract from the agent since he did not explicitly become guarantor for interest. But when the non-Jew does not redeem except from the agent, and the agent redeems from the lender and says the non-Jew is silent and did not give interest as stipulated — the lender is not obligated to return collateral unless he gives what he stipulated, for he can say I have no dealings with the non-Jew, only with you; and specifically by default; but if the lending Jew explicitly said you shall be my agent, he must give collateral without interest, only no loss in principal — end quote. And it is clear from his words that wherever he explicitly stipulated about interest, obviously he must pay, for the loan body was on the collateral; and see below siman 170 s.k. 7, 8; and Bach further wrote in his conclusion that Tur in the name of Rabeinu Yehudah wrote the lender need not return collateral but takes interest from the collateral or sells it to the agent Jew for principal and interest; and nevertheless when he mentioned the non-Jew's name and recognized he was silent, he should not take interest even from the collateral, for he knew and waived, and must return collateral without interest, etc.`,
    '14#ד': `"Rather the loan's beginning must be for the non-Jew's need." Meaning he should say he borrows for the non-Jew's need; for even though it is known he borrows for the non-Jew's need, he should not say he will pay principal and interest; and likewise is explicit in Rashba's responsum: even if he borrowed it for the non-Jew's need, when he told Shimon he will pay principal and interest — it turns out Shimon borrows and Reuven lends; and even though the collateral belongs to the non-Jew, Reuven claims from Shimon and Shimon is liable to him; and it is close in my eyes that one who says in such language commits fixed ribbit even if the collateral belonged to the non-Jew — for this is as Shimon borrows from Reuven with interest and lends to the non-Jew — end quote; and he brings Darkei Moshe. And even though it is permitted for a Jew to be even a guarantor-contractor for the non-Jew where there is collateral, as below siman 170 seif 2 in the Hagahah — that case differs, for the loan's beginning was to the non-Jew. However I have seen practiced in many places that the Jew says at loan time he will pay principal and interest; and it appears they hold specifically where the collateral is not good to lend the full loan amount equaling principal and interest even over a long time — then it appears the lender relies on the Jew, and in such a case it is forbidden to say so. And in such a case I have seen practiced that the Jew gives the lender a promissory note for permission as security. But where collateral is good, the Jew's words neither raise nor lower; and since he lends for the non-Jew's need there is no concern; nevertheless one should be careful.`,
    '15#_': `The lender is not, etc. Bach and Taz wrote: this is specifically by default; but if the lender said you shall be my agent, etc. — obviously the agent can tell the lender do not sell it because the non-Jew is silent, as above and similar. Pri Megadim wrote: it seems specifically in such a case where the agent brought collateral with him at loan time as we said; but if he did not bring collateral with him but the non-Jew sent him to bring money and afterward would give his collateral — then responsibility for collateral was never on the agent, only the agent was the Jew's agent as above siman 13; the agent can say do not sell it lest it harm me because the non-Jew is silent, etc.; and if he sold it and the agent came to harm thereby the lender must compensate — it seems to me — end quote.`,
    '18#ג': `"And if they stipulated from the outset," etc., "and it is possible," etc. The Rabbi wrote this from his reasoning per his words in Darkei Moshe; and I wonder, since they stipulated from the outset that he must return and give it to him; and likewise where obligated to return by custom which is as explicit stipulation — if so this is not a sale and forbidden to take interest, as Rashba wrote in a responsum and the Rabbi brought below siman 174; and likewise explicit in Mordechai that it is permitted only because since he is not obligated to return collateral it is a sale, as he wrote that by default he intended for permission even without explicitly stating so his intent was complete removal, that if he wishes he need not return collateral at all but relies he will return and allow redemption and accept principal and interest, etc.; and likewise in Tosafot siman 303: in actual stipulation one cannot find remedy to act permissibly; but in assurance and trust one can act permissibly, etc.; and even though Darkei Moshe wrote Tosafot discusses only where he fixed interest as below — it is not so, for examining Tosafot shows no distinction between fixed and unfixed; and likewise Beit Yosef that complete trust in a complete condition is forbidden as stated explicitly in the Gemara regarding one who sold a house and said when I have money return it — forbidden until here; and explained below simanim 172 and 174. But in truth it seems the custom is simply to lend on non-Jewish collaterals and lenders return collaterals to the Jew — they do so willingly as if returned and sold by default, but not obligated; and even if obligated, because they relied on Rosh perek Kol Sha'ah in the name of Baal HaItur that a non-Jew's collateral in a Jew's hand with interest — permitted to pledge to another Jew at that interest even without presenting before the non-Jew, and we do not say the Jew takes interest, for he did not acquire except where he said from now — end quote; and Beit Yosef brought it; and from Rosh's words there it implies agreement with Baal HaItur; and likewise in Ramzim there; and therefore it discusses where he does not sell to him and the second Jew is obligated to return to the first to redeem — stipulated thus or custom; but if complete sale or pledging by default as one who said I sell to you — by default intends for permission — even if non-Jew said acquire from now likewise permitted; only truly the Jew's; permitted to sell to Jew and Jew returns and sells as below; or discusses only collateral in second Jew's hand and nevertheless permitted since non-Jew did not say acquire from now unless redeemed by set time — behold in non-Jew's possession; and since the second lending Jew's primary reliance is on collateral, the ribbit he takes is truly from non-Jewish collateral; and so Darkei Moshe seif 92: Rosh perek Kol Sha'ah discusses as explained — "and it seems Rosh follows his reasoning who wrote in responsum he need not sell but hands it over," etc.; therefore forbidden when saying from now; but Rabbeinu Tam who permitted because he sells — even in such case permitted — two sales — it seems to me — end quote. And now it is resolved what Rosh wrote perek Avodah Zarah that it is permitted only when the Jew sells collateral to the second Jew — unlike Beit Yosef and Bach who understood the rulings contradict; rather Avodah Zarah discusses collateral in the Jew's possession, such as if he said acquire from now or the Jew sees the non-Jew intends to remove it; and likewise Rosh in responsum permitted pledging collateral from the non-Jew in his hand to a Jew even if he does not sell but hands over on condition to take interest from the non-Jew — does not contradict what he wrote in his rulings, for there the reason since he said take interest from the non-Jew he automatically departs from collateral and therefore need not explicitly say he sold it — see there klal 108 siman 4 explained thus; and as Tur wrote Rosh's responsum does not contradict his rulings but is a novel law unlike Beit Yosef and Bach and other Acharonim. However all this when collateral is in Jew's possession — said acquire from now or sees intent to remove or custom after a year authority to sell — otherwise obviously permitted in every manner; and what most poskim and Mechaber wrote requiring the Jew explicitly say I sell it to you (unlike Mordechai) — also discusses if in Jew's possession (and see responsum Maharam Prague siman 328). And now we hold like most poskim, except our custom follows Baal HaItur and Rosh perek Kol Sha'ah whom none disagree with thus — it seems clear to me.`,
    '18#ד': `"Also if the borrower and lender wish," etc. And it seems here there must be explicit "I sell it to you in a complete sale" everywhere that is for all; otherwise it is not in the meaning that he sold it since it is still in the borrower's hand; and so it appears from the Rabbi's words "provided the collateral from now on be on the second lender's responsibility," etc.; and likewise per what is stated above that if collaterals are in the non-Jew's hand — did not say acquire from now — permitted to lend from a Jew with interest — here too must transfer collaterals in every manner that they be the second Jew's in every manner as collateral — it seems to me.`,
    '18#ה': `"For by Torah law," etc. And as stated in CM siman 198.`,
    '18#ו': `"Even though some doubt," etc. Meaning Mahariik in shoresh 119 — it appears from his words that transferring his debt on the collateral does not help; and likewise Darkei Moshe; and likewise Maharshal responsum siman 52. And to me it appears Mahariik's main intent is only that a person cannot transfer a debt he has on the non-Jew even with kinyan and agav and standing on land as Mordechai perek Mi SheMet wrote in the name of Maharam's responsum; and possibly it discusses where the debt exceeds collateral value — in such case transferring collateral does not help unless he transfers the debt from the collaterals; and a non-Jew's debt is not acquired by kinyan. But certainly Mahariik agrees when he transfers the collateral it is acquired everywhere — it seems clear to me. And see there; examine (and thereby resolved well what Beit Yosef strained in Mahariik's words — see there).`,
    '18#ט': `"You exempt the non-Jew," etc. Maimoniyos responsum siman 68 wrote in the name of R. Meir: when the Jew goes to the non-Jew's house and guarantees and says to them, the debt you owe me — transfer to this my fellow; and when you transfer to him you are exempt from me; and he wrote all this and brought them Beit Yosef; and it seems the same law when he does not go to them and exempts the non-Jew immediately that he cannot sue him in any matter — only the second Jew extracts from them what he can — permitted; and as Tur's words imply.`,
    '20#ב': `"And for the first reasoning," etc. And one can say unlike above seif 10 that if a Jew redeems it is forbidden — for there immediately when the Jew gives collateral and borrows on it it appears as borrowing for a Jew's need; or there since he pledged against his will certainly he did not transfer it; but here one can say he transferred it. So Beit Yosef regarding the law of that seif; and thereby also no question from above seif 9 forbidden when lender knew collateral belongs to Jew — for there he borrows for Jew's need; and likewise the Acharonim.`,
    '21#_': `On the non-Jew's responsibility, etc. Specifically we require the non-Jew lend them from the Jew's hand and the non-Jew accepts that if the debts are lost he bends to the Jew as any borrower's law; but if he accepted responsibility like other guardians, and even obligated himself for accidents when they are lost as long as they stand intact — forbidden and he does not become borrower from now. Beit Yosef in the name of Beit HaTalmud; and Darkei Moshe brought it.`,
    '24#א': `"Guardian over his property." Bach wrote: specifically over all his property; unlike when he did not become overseer or guardian except to lend his money with interest, as he forbids "take your wage and lend my money with interest," etc.; and from the students of Rashba and Beit Yosef who wrote regarding "take your wage" that in his craft thus it is permitted — it appears explicitly not over all property; and possibly even Bach means only when the matter is not well known; and so it appears from the end of his words concluding because not everyone knows he became guardian or overseer of the non-Jew since he does not conduct other business of the non-Jew's property.`,
    '25#ב': `"Without oath." For he does not abandon a permitted path and eat forbidden matter; and we say he certainly did everything he could permissibly.`,
    '25#ג': `"I do not know," etc. And the same if he says "I do not know" — he must swear heses. Beit Yosef in the name of Rashba's responsa; and likewise Mechaber below siman 177 seif 1; and likewise Pri Megadim; and Bach and Semag CM siman 82 s.k. 34, unlike those who disagree in Hagahah siman 177 there.`,
    '25#ה': `"Such as he had collateral," etc. It implies even if he claims "you lent me one hundred with fixed ribbit on collateral" and the other responds "I acted permissibly" — exempt without oath; and so Beit Yosef implies; and all the more if he claims "I left collateral for interest" and the other claims it was done permissibly from the outset. And that Rosh and Tur wrote above seif 18 s.k. 54 regarding claiming collateral at fixed ribbit and the other responds you said it belongs to the non-Jew and he denies — lender swears, etc. — one can distinguish: here permitted because we do not abandon permitted path; there he did nothing permissibly but relied on you — essence of permit from borrower's word and borrower denies, therefore must swear — it seems to me.`,
    '25#ו': `"Swears and is exempt." Puzzling — Mordechai wrote simply the lender is believed without oath, and it implies even oral loan; but Beit Yosef explained this is specifically with document or collateral in hand; but if oral loan obviously not believed to extract even with oath — end quote; and he brings Darkei Moshe; and I do not know why obviously, for one can say from reason a person does not make himself wicked he is not believed; and likewise Semag negative precepts 193 folio 63 side 2 in the name of R. Yaakov: if he denies and says it is ribbit loan, even though if he wishes he exempts himself by saying I paid or I do not know — not believed, for a person does not make himself wicked — end quote; Beit Yosef brought below siman 177 and established in Shulchan Aruch there seif 12; and necessarily oral loan is discussed, otherwise no migo; and even though Shulchan Aruch there did not explain oral loan is discussed, since his words are taken from Semag it implies as Semag; and further whence does Beit Yosef know to explain Mordechai's words not like plain meaning and not like Semag in the manner discussed; and likewise Mahariwe siman 37 wrote simply borrower is not believed because a person does not make himself wicked; and Bach followed Beit Yosef and explained document or collateral is discussed — not in his language and strained to distinguish; and further Darkei Moshe seif 6 regarding this law above seif 13 "to your first words I believe," etc. — brought Mordechai's words and wrote: and likewise Semag — even where there is migo borrower is not believed, a person does not make himself wicked — end quote; puzzling; and Pri Megadim also wrote here borrower swears and is exempt even though he makes himself and the lender wicked — presumption money is preferable to follow him through oath — end quote; and below siman 177 seif 12 wrote even though if he wishes he exempts himself — not believed with oath, a person does not make himself wicked — end quote; matters contradict — puzzling.`,
    '25#ז': `"And if there is a document," etc. And one can say unlike above siman 162, there one cannot say he did not abandon permitted path — here there is certainly prohibition; but there it did not enter his mind wheat would become expensive to be careful to have wheat at loan time; and likewise Rosh distinguishes explicitly klal 108 siman 7.`,
    '25#ח': `"Swears and takes." Semag wrote CM there s.k. 33: specifically here where he admits ribbit exists but says done permissibly — swears and takes by promissory note as law of anyone who swears on document and takes. But if lender stands on document and says I do not know any ribbit exists — need not swear before giving money by promissory note but pays and afterward sues and administers heses; and likewise Bach there; and in this siman he mixed matters and contradicted himself — CM there see; and nevertheless puzzling, for this law the Rabbi wrote "swears and takes" is recorded in all Shulchan Aruch books as Beit Yosef's reasoning from Mordechai; and likewise Darkei Moshe wrote: Beit Yosef wrote if lender comes to extract and borrower says it is ribbit — if lender has presumption such as collateral or document, lender swears and takes, etc. — end quote; and Beit Yosef wrote explicitly the opposite per Mordechai: with collateral or document he takes without oath when claiming "I acted permissibly with you" — see there; puzzling; and see Pri Megadim and responsa Ma'aseh Binyamin siman 67; and Beit Yosef further on laws relevant here.`,
    '26#_': `Permitted. It seems to me it discusses a matter where the non-Jew relies on collateral alone — such as collateral is good or he tells the non-Jew I am not obligated to you at all but your responsibility is only on the collateral; otherwise not better than if he gave collateral to pledge with the non-Jew in possession above s.k. 61 forbidden; and in this Kol Bo discusses in the name of Maharih; and Beit Yosef wrote "but Kol Bo," etc. — understood he disagrees; and Maharshal also challenged Kol Bo; and Semag brought it CM siman 73 — see there; and in my humble opinion as stated.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '2#_': `The interest into the first Jew's hand. Not specifically interest but principal too — meaning after the non-Jew received from the first Jew and gave to the second Jew, he told the first Jew: be in my place to receive my money from the second Jew, principal and interest; and the first Jew did so. This is explained in Hagahos Mordechai that Beit Yosef brings: the Baraisa "they presented before a non-Jew — permitted" discusses where the non-Jew told the Jew receive my money from the first Jew and he is my guardian — but you cannot sue him except through me — and permitted even if he gives interest to the Jew — end quote; in the Hagahah there it does not say interest but simply "receive my money" — meaning even principal; and it is a clear matter there is a scribal error — how can the second Jew be guardian when he returns money to the first Jew and gives him interest, for this is the sugya's topic "and I will pay you," etc., implying the first Jew receives even in the conclusion; and likewise proven in Hagahos Mordechai's conclusion that he is only a depositary, etc. — see there; and this applies if he receives principal too; but if he receives only interest what novelty is there. Rather the main point as my teacher z"l wrote in the name of great ones: there is a scribal error and it should read: he told the first Jew receive my money from the second Jew, etc. — meaning as I stated: after the non-Jew received and gave to the second Jew he says thus to the first; and he need not mention this since it refers to the beginning where the second Jew told the first "and I will pay you" — and this applies also to the conclusion that in this formula it is permitted if the non-Jew gave and took by hand first. But in Tur it is written "even if he gives interest to the first Jew," etc. — implying it does not discuss appointing the first Jew as guardian to receive principal from the second; nevertheless for the law Tur too holds it permitted when the non-Jew gave and took. And what Hagahos Mordechai wrote "you cannot sue him except through me" — means there shall be no responsibility on you; and even though below seif 23 it is explained forbidden because of marit ayin — I am here where the loan did not go Jew to Jew but only after the first Jew completely departed; and see what is written in the Hagahah shortly on this.`,
    '3#א': `"Place them on the ground." In the Gemara it states on the ground and exempt; and Beit Yosef wrote the Tur holds "and exempt" is not specifically required; and one can question whence the Tur to explain thus — behold in CM siman 120 when he says throw my debt and he threw, the borrower is still responsible until he says throw my debt and exempt; and let us say here too it is specifically required, and whoever did not say so is forbidden since still on the Jew's responsibility — and this is certainly no distinction between "throw" and "place on the ground." And it seems the Tur holds since the main prohibition here is receiving from the Jew's hand — it appears as the first Jew's agent giving ribbit to the non-Jew — and since he does not take from his hand but from the ground, this does not apply; and what they said in the Gemara "and exempt" — they did not mean exempt from responsibility which we do not care about, but to exclude that he not leave them on condition he return and take them — then it would be as if he did not place them; therefore he said and exempt — meaning exempt yourself from here onward and the money shall remain placed; and this is what Tur and Shulchan Aruch wrote "and he did and withdrew" — for apparently "and he withdrew" is superfluous; and per my explanation it is resolved: specifically we require he leave there; but as long as he stands there it is as if still in his hand, for perhaps he will return and take; and if so Tur's words are exactly the Talmud's words and there is no "not specifically required" in the Talmud.`,
    '3#ב': `"To receive them from the first Jew through him." My teacher z"l challenged this — behold it was already explained in seif 1 that saying give them to him and exempt and I will stipulate with him, etc. is forbidden; and one can answer: there is different, for there he said give them and exempt — exempt only after giving, so at giving time there was prohibition; unlike here where the non-Jew explicitly tells the first Jew: behold responsibility for the money is from today on me and you shall be my guardian — as I mentioned in seif 2 in Hagahos Mordechai — automatically there is permission at giving time. And this is explained in what Rama wrote "and the first Jew is only like the non-Jew's agent"; and even though in seif 23 he wrote regarding guardian forbidden because of marit ayin — I am here where the Jew made the loan with the non-Jew's consent, only that Baal Hagahos Mordechai wrote this explicitly on the Baraisa in what it said "and if they presented before a non-Jew — permitted" — discussing making the first Jew guardian; and nevertheless we do not find permission in the Gemara in explaining the Baraisa except placing on ground or giving and taking by hand; and Rama wrote here that even if the Jew received from the first Jew's hand it is permitted since he made the first guardian — and there is no one to answer this matter; and this Hagahah is not halakhah; and even though Levush followed him, there is no proof he delved deeply.`,
    '5#_': `It discusses where the Jew coerces, etc. This is Hagahos Maimoniyos' view that Beit Yosef wrote distinguishes unsecured debt from collateral as Beit Yosef wrote; and it is in Maimoniyos' responsum to judges siman 33 in the name of Maharam; and proof from Tosafot who forbid borrowing through a non-Jew — therefore they distinguish unsecured from collateral; but other poskim such as Semag and Semak explain Tosafot discusses where responsibility is on the Jew; if so no distinction between unsecured and collateral; and likewise Beit Yosef in the name of Rosh and Tur that they do not distinguish thus; and see what I wrote in seif 12 on this law.`,
    '6#_': `Even though the non-Jew gave the money, etc. The reason: even though we hold there is no agency for a non-Jew above seif 1 — that is for leniency; but here for stringency we say there is agency for a non-Jew. So Rashi's view; and "some are lenient" is Rabbeinu Tam's view that even for stringency we do not say agency for a non-Jew, even in unsecured debt without collateral — so Beit Yosef wrote for Rosh's view; and Tur brought Hagahos Maimoniyos that they disagree and hold even for Rabbeinu Tam it is not permitted except with collateral and not unsecured; and see what I wrote in seif 12 for the law.`,
    '7#_': `"And some are lenient." This is Rabbeinu Tam's view above — even if the lender knew it is for the Jew's sake, as Rama wrote in seif 9 even if they are his books, etc.; and likewise explained in Rosh's responsum klal 108 din 18 according to Rabbeinu Tam.`,
    '10#א': `A non-Jew who pledged, etc. Specifically regarding a non-Jew who borrowed from a Jew on collateral we say in seif 9 it is permitted because by default he transferred it; but here certainly he did not transfer it, for he took it against his will; and if the Jew redeems it, it is as if he sent his agent the non-Jew to borrow with ribbit — forbidden according to Rashi above; therefore if the pledged party did not know the pledger would pledge it with a Jew and agency does not apply at all — permitted even according to Rashi.`,
    '10#ב': `Debt with interest and he transferred it, etc. This is simple: if he already owed the non-Jew principal and interest he can accept on himself and take from the Jew that interest too; except there in Rashba's responsum it discusses where the Jew owed the non-Jew principal and interest had not yet accrued at the hour the non-Jew transferred to another Jew; and know that if he transferred in a complete sale to another Jew obviously complete prohibition (only) even to take from the non-Jew; rather it discusses transfer as security, and when the non-Jew wishes he can remove the Jew with money; if so it is only like collateral and we should have permitted taking interest even from the Jew since the money is essentially the non-Jew's on this — he wrote forbidden because of ribbit stringency and concluded with language "and similar" implying; but in truth it is not so; and thereby we understand this Hagahah — not as it appears discussing complete acquisition which is forbidden even from the non-Jew since from that hour it is Jew's money and it is as borrowing from his fellow on condition the non-Jew gives interest — certainly forbidden.`,
    '11#ב': `"But he cannot acquire for the non-Jew," etc. Beit Yosef brought in Mordechai's name in Rabeinu Yehudah's language: if the non-Jew told Reuven place collateral in my place and the Jew took his collateral and told Shimon this is the non-Jew's collateral or said I transfer to you in his place and Shimon agreed — permitted to receive interest from the pledging Jew who is Shimon's agent to lend to the non-Jew; and likewise when he transfers collateral; and if Reuven denies, Shimon swears and is exempt — end quote. Beit Yosef on what he said "this is the non-Jew's collateral" — the permission is even though collateral is the Jew's because collateral has guarantor law, for he does not sell unless he first demands payment, etc. I wondered how a holy mouth could say this, for certainly the lender's primary reliance is on the Jew's collateral which truly belongs to the Jew — what does demanding the Jew first help; payment comes from the Jew either way; unlike a Jew guarantor for a non-Jew where if the non-Jew demands first payment does not come from the Jew — unlike here. But it appears to me this is not Mordechai's path; so it states there and in Hagahos Ashiri and Rosh's responsum klal 108 siman 5: a non-Jew told Reuven lend me money on my collateral and Reuven went and borrowed from Shimon on the non-Jew's collateral; or the non-Jew asked him place collateral in my place and he told Shimon this is the non-Jew's collateral or said I transfer to you in his place — permitted for Shimon to take interest from Reuven who is Shimon's agent to lend to the non-Jew; and likewise when he transferred collateral — end quote. Beit Yosef and my teacher z"l understood Mordechai's statement "this is the non-Jew's collateral" refers to what precedes — the non-Jew asked him place collateral in his place — and on this Mordechai gave two divisions; and they labored to find a reason for permission when the Jew left his collateral in his place — heaven forbid to say this is a general permission, for it is not like a guarantor — here is prohibition even if the lender thinks collateral is the non-Jew's he automatically has payment in hand without coercion even through the agent on the non-Jew who will say behold you have collateral needing only warning; and all the more truly it belongs to the Jew and the agent transgresses "before the blind." My teacher z"l also wrote even without collateral permitted, etc. — he was not precise, for without collateral it is worse since lender's primary reliance is on the non-Jew — end quote; it appears complete prohibition. And what Ran wrote even without collateral permitted — means non-Jewish collateral; rather it refers to Mordechai's first statement: non-Jew told Reuven lend on my collateral and Shimon did so — Mordechai says two paths: first borrowed on non-Jew's collateral and says this is the non-Jew's collateral without lying; second says I transfer, etc. — in both permitted; therefore concludes permitted to take from Reuven who is Shimon's agent — meaning the first division; but the second has no relevance; and on the second path concludes "and likewise when he transferred collateral" — this is clear as the sun for they did not examine properly in their haste. And on Mordechai transferring for the non-Jew Beit Yosef wondered — we hold there is no acquisition for a non-Jew in perek HaRibbit (71a); therefore he explained at least prohibition for the agent only permission for the lender — very strained; if so poskim would not be silent and why not mention at least prohibition for the agent lest he do so with a Jew. My teacher z"l wrote possibly to resolve: meaning he transfers in a way the non-Jew acquires — such as through admission he admits it belongs to the non-Jew like the case of Isur the convert, etc. — not clear to me; that admission case where he admitted the money is not his does not apply here at all, for certainly regarding money we say a person is believed about his liability and admission of litigant is like hundred witnesses even though we know he lies, as Shulchan Aruch CM siman 113 seif 2; but this certainly does not help to remove prohibition through his lie — consider if a Jew borrows from his fellow, gives ribbit, and says I admit this money belongs to a non-Jew — can permission come through this famous lie. I again saw in Maimoniyos Mishpatim siman 31 regarding selling a non-Jew's debt — admission does not help in the name of Baal HaItur; the initial challenge does not exist per my view here — not that the non-Jew acquired this collateral and the lender acquired from the non-Jew; certainly no acquisition for a non-Jew; rather here the pledgor gives the lender a path of sale for this collateral saying I transfer this thing to be yours on this right that if so-and-so non-Jew gives you principal and fixed interest you return the collateral — this is exactly as one sells to his fellow for a non-Jew and stipulates conditions; likewise here; and there is no loan on the collateral — the pledgor is the lender's borrower and collateral is security. So it appears to me.`,
    '13#ב': `Since he first admitted it is not his. Maharshal wrote on this in a responsum siman 99: I wonder why Rosh considered this admission of litigant — why not say they schemed so he would lend at his distress and this is not in "remnant of Israel shall not do wrong" except a estimable person or even not estimable but wants to profit from another's money; but where known he was pressed there is no such category; and if clarification by witnesses does not help, nevertheless scheming helps even though he has presumption in collateral — nevertheless he comes with claim of perhaps, etc. — end quote; and this is included in what Rama wrote afterward "however if it became known," etc. — per the court's eyes.`,
    '14#_': `"And some say the borrower must pay from his pocket." This is Avi HaEzri's view. It appears his explanation: if he wants the lender to give him collateral and he does not wish to pay all interest he fixed — he must pay from his pocket or leave the collateral; but if he mentioned the non-Jew's name he must give collateral without interest. Thereby resolved what Beit Yosef wrote that Rosh disagrees with this law — even when collateral responsibility is on the agent forbidden, all the more to take interest from borrower if non-Jew silent — end quote. And it seems to me there is no dispute; Avi HaEzri too holds responsibility is on the agent, only the agent wants to extract collateral without interest — on this he said pay from pocket or leave collateral with lender. And I wonder at Beit Yosef — behold Rosh wrote shortly that if lender does not wish to wait in selling collateral lest he lose in waiting, the agent must add collateral from himself; if so a fortiori the agent has no power to compel lender to give collateral without interest — simply this is not in responsibility category; responsibility is if lost or stolen from agent the loss is lender's; but here lender does not wish to give collateral from his hand until he received principal and interest — in this all agree. My teacher z"l challenged — behold the first view Shulchan Aruch wrote borrower does not pay from pocket is written in Mordechai in the name of Aviasaf; and Aviasaf is Avi HaEzri — Rabeinu Yehudah; and from this he entered explanations and distinctions; examining Mordechai shows no difficulty — he wrote laws in the name of Rivba and among them this law borrower does not pay from pocket; and concluded on some of Rivba's laws there is response; afterward wrote Aviasaf meant Aviasaf wrote one should challenge some of Rivba's laws — clearly he said this on this law and disagrees as we saw in his commentary. He further challenged: since main permission is the Jew sends his fellow to lend his money to the non-Jew — why must he pay from pocket if non-Jew silent; and he extended to answer. And in my humble opinion it is no difficulty — even though he is his agent, since he entered the matter he accepted not to harm him in relying on collateral; therefore must ensure no loss — laws of causative damage; when non-Jew silent certainly no interest and he should have informed this non-Jew is silent; therefore it seems if agent did not know this non-Jew is coerced and learned afterward — no obligation on him and sender must give collateral without interest — why lose since he did not sin — so it seems. Levush wrote: it seems specifically when agent brought collateral at loan time; but if he did not bring collateral at loan time but non-Jew sent to bring money and afterward would give collateral — then responsibility was never on agent, only agent was Jew's agent as I wrote above; agent can say do not sell lest it harm me because non-Jew silent; if sold and agent harmed, lender must compensate — so it seems — end quote; he was not precise — Rosh explicitly discusses sending to bring collateral from non-Jew on lender's responsibility as Tur cited explicitly in his name; and nevertheless concluded if agent fears non-Jew he adds collateral to Jew as Shulchan Aruch cited seif 15 — end quote; his words have no place in my view; and whenever agent knew non-Jew is coerced and did not inform lender he cannot afterward extract collateral from lender without interest for he caused his own loss — so it seems.`,
    '17#ב': `For the interest accrues on the others. Meaning that interest the payers profit falls on the others to complete it.`,
    '18#א': `"I sell you all power," etc. Levush wrote: for this reason it is permitted for a Jew to pledge a non-Jew's collateral with another Jew — he explains more than what is pledged with him and interest shall be only what the non-Jew gives him; for we say he sold all collateral to the second and what he lent this one more he lent free without interest — end quote. I greatly wondered at these words — how can you say the surplus is interest-free loan; immediately when the second Jew fixed with him at less than all the gold the non-Jew promised, the first profited that surplus; and if he did not take principal from the second only as given to the non-Jew he would not give such interest — why give the second afterward; necessarily for what he lent more than he lent the non-Jew; and there is complete ribbit; and even if no loss, there is ribbit from preventing the agent's profit; and though this is obvious I bring clear proof from Talmud perek HaRibbit (65a): Rava Pappa said my document is permitted — Rashi: he was a date-broker and sold cheap in Tishrei at Nisan rate and waited until Nisan — why? I do not lose, I could store until Nisan, I do not need money, I do a favor for the buyer. Rava Sheshet told Rava Pappa: why do you follow yourself — follow them, the buyers; if they had money they would take now as now; now they have no money they take at future rate — and halakhah follows Rava Sheshet as stated beginning siman 173. Behold the buyer has no loss — if he wished he could hold until payment time worth that amount; even if buyer holds until that time there is prohibition though no loss, since one side could profit and he did not — we consider him a paid watchman; likewise here though borrower has no real loss since non-Jew gives that interest from smaller sum as from larger — nevertheless if he took from Jew smaller sum he would profit and he forfeits that profit for extra loan — a fortiori in Rava Pappa's document which is sale where ribbit prohibition is rabbinic they were stringent; all the more here loan where prohibition is Torah — therefore it appears fixed ribbit extractable by judges; do not honor rabbi over colleague; let this permit be suppressed and not done among Jews.`,
    '18#ג': `"And if they stipulated from the outset in it." It is Torah and I need to learn — for this ruling seems puzzling, since main permission in this law is we grasp it as sale; and any sale with condition he is obligated to return when he returns the money as stated perek HaRibbit brought at beginning siman 174 — sold field and said when you return to me, etc. — did not acquire; likewise here if they stipulated there is no sale; and so is Tur and Shulchan Aruch's language in this seif — wrote the second wished to return and take from the first, etc. — implies not that he wished not; he should have written stipulation helps — great novelty. Further proof from Maimoniyos Mishpatim siman 30: it happened once Rabbenu Tam had collateral from a non-Jew and needed money and to give collateral to lend on it; he asked how to be permitted — the non-Jew borrows from you and owes you interest, etc.; he answered I sell you all collateral and do not trouble me further if you wish and I exempt you and you are not obligated to return collateral like this Rabbenu did — end quote. Behold on the contrary he had to say he is not obligated to return collateral; and if there is permission when they stipulated he could also stipulate — why diminish his right for free; who asked this of him; rather certainly main permission is from this; and so Rosh's words responsum klal 108 siman 5: we can say if the broker told the Jew this collateral of the non-Jew — acquire the collateral for you; and if the non-Jew comes give it to redeem; I have no dealings with you nor you with me — possibly permitted, etc. — end quote; behold he insisted specifically no dealings regarding returning collateral to redeem; all this refutes Rama's ruling here whose source is Tosafot siman 303; and I abbreviated its language: asked Reuven who fixed with non-Jew borrower on Nisan 100 dinar for 112 by year — 12 dinar interest per hundred by Sivan — came to Shimon to pay half the debt — 50 half principal — and pay interest on all as from non-Jew as long as he does not return principal and interest and authority in Reuven's hand to return after month or two months — how act permissibly; answered all interest fixed with non-Jew is considered Reuven's principal lent to non-Jew — all 112 dinar are Jew's principal; Mordechai further wrote if Jew has collateral from non-Jew with interest permitted for another Jew to redeem thus — first sells to second so interest from here on is on his collateral; if after time first wishes to return and redeem — permitted to give second principal and interest accruing on collateral while in his hand; second returns and sells to first to be his as initially; brought the case siman 174 gift that returns — not sale; per this wrote no remedy to transfer fixed document with non-Jew to fellow for weekly interest — since debt fixed with non-Jew all debt principal and interest is Jew's money; turns out weekly ribbit he takes not from non-Jew but from Jew's money as wages for advancing money before due date — certainly if he wished to buy debt completely now for less than written amount calculating interest until fixed time — such case completely permitted, etc. But if seller does not intend complete sale but to return and buy when he has money — must act so he is not completely secure in complete condition to return but secure he will not change his promise. Now explain the question: if he wishes buy entire debt in Sivan for 102 dinar — all his from now; if they wish the manner in the question — redeem only first principal on condition second agrees to return and sell if bought in Sivan for 102 and returns in Av for 104 for what accrued while debt owned — end quote. From this Rama learned only where he first fixed interest it is forbidden afterward to make complete condition; but without fixing permitted with complete condition. And fixed-interest law Rama wrote at end of Hagahah; and must understand Tosafot's view — since truly even without prior fixing permission is only through sale first and end; and since obligated to return it was not sale from outset; if Tosafot had some distinction he would have written it; and it appears his view: without fixing sale comes only to remove first lender's right to what accrues weekly from non-Jew; but need not transfer anything — what does he transfer, it is something not yet in existence, interest born each week; therefore only self-removal applies even if he removes for a time and stipulates if he has money he returns and sells; but with fixing then sale comes to transfer to second what is already the first Jew's complete right; therefore sale must be such second is not obligated to return to first if he has money — for this nullifies sale meant to give second complete acquisition. So I explain; but we already proved from other poskim that even on plain non-Jewish collateral sale is required such that he not be obligated to return when he has money; and I wonder Tosafot did not bring them. In any case it appears to be stringent as poskim we cited stated; and every conscientious person should act with second lender's willingness to return and redeem if redeemed without non-Jew's knowledge — so it seems.`,
    '19#א': `"Lend me money on these collaterals," etc., "and the money is for the Jew's need." Tur did not write this but "lend me money," etc.; and Beit Yosef wrote this needs explanation because it was challenged what prohibition is in the matter. And in Derishah he wrote Tur's words mean: if he lends on collateral one hundred and wants to pledge in second Jew's hand for fifty — collaterals still in his possession and he returns and borrows on them for his need — forbidden; and he was not precise — Tosafot siman 303 explicitly wrote no distinction between selling entire debt or part; likewise collateral — one matter. And in Perishah he wrote prohibition because collateral was acquired by Jew from non-Jew and must sell specifically to second Jew; therefore if he said lend on these non-Jewish collaterals there is no sale. Darkei Moshe wrote custom is lenient even if he said lend me; and likewise Mordechai perek HaRibbit practical law permitted even if he did not say I remove myself and did not say he sold it — as one who said by default intends for permission; and since he said this collateral stands for me from the non-Jew for such amount lend on it thus and from today accept interest accruing — permitted — end quote.`,
    '20#_': `A Jew's collateral, etc., permitted to lend, etc. This is Rosh's view; and even though above seif 7 wrote specifically lender did not know for Jew's sake — and that too is Rosh's view; here differs because known the non-Jew borrows for his own need.`,
    '25#א': `Believed without oath. For a person does not abandon a permitted path and do forbidden matter as we say regarding prozbul CM siman 67 seif 33 — a person is believed to say I had a prozbul and it was lost.`,
    '25#ב': `"And if he responds I do not know," etc. Beit Yosef wrote the same in claims "there never was such matter"; and per this resolved what CM siman 82 seif 12 wrote regarding claiming this debt is ribbit — must swear heses — meaning claims I do not know; and nevertheless must understand why truly obligated to swear in these claims more than claim I took permissibly — difference is only acquisition alone without obligation from that; since if he admitted he took he still has claim to exempt that he took permissibly; CM siman 93 seif 9 heses only on claim where admission obligates money, etc.; a fortiori here even if admits can exempt saying I took permissibly; appears insufficient to say I took permissibly without specifying permission how — lest he not know how to make permit; example CM siman 67 Rosh never saw anyone succeed with claim prozbul I had and lost because they did not know prozbul at all; further proof Rosh klal 108 siman 7 Reuven borrowed from Shimon 100 gakshish, etc. — lender claimed borrower had gakshish; ruled we do not say he abandoned permitted path because it did not enter mind gakshish would become expensive — unlike prozbul, etc. — end quote. Likewise in all permits possibly he thought matter would not need permit and thereby done forbidden; therefore if he explicitly says done permissibly as we can investigate if matter where no concern it did not occur to him he would need permit — therefore if says I do not know or silent there is this concern and he swears he does not know or is silent. Thereby resolved Beit Yosef's challenge why believe claim I do not know with migo I took through non-Jew — answered we do not say migo to exempt from oath; not so — CM siman 93 explicitly Rosh says we say migo here; and per my words it fits — possibly he is not clear on that permit; and per this path appears what Rama wrote afterward "and likewise in other claims," etc. — meaning he specifies permission how, not simply; and even regarding prozbul they said specifically must say so, not suffice "I sue permissibly"; and even for one not wanting to explain thus saying simply I took permissibly suffices — nevertheless practically one should act thus now, for we see many breach ribbit prohibition and it is not considered anything until almost inapplicable to say he did not abandon permitted path — he does not care to act permissibly at all; and this we learn from Rosh changing law for shemitah and not giving credibility to his generation saying prozbul I had though explicit in Talmudic sages' days — likewise ribbit in our case must at least investigate how done permissibly; and if judge sees unclear he administers oath — so it seems.`,
    '25#ג': `"And if there is a document in the lender's hand — swears and takes." In Mordechai's words Beit Yosef brings it says he takes without oath; but Rama decided in any case he is not presumed from the document like one presumed in collateral taking without oath; and it appears thus proven from what is stated perek HaRibbit (62a) all agree a document standing to collect is not as collected; nevertheless the document helps that he swears and takes; and Rama learned from CM siman 82 seif 10 we mentioned.`,
  },
};
